{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== AC4: THE COMPLETE [attr-defined] LIST FROM THE ANNOTATION PASS, AND EACH ONE'S RESOLUTION ===\nMeasured BEFORE resolving anything (plan step 3), by annotating every bare/Any client|transport\nparam in the core at once and running mypy src/rebar. FOUR [attr-defined] errors, and sixteen\n[assignment] errors. That is the whole list; nothing is omitted.\n\n--- CLASS A: 4 x [attr-defined] — ALL FOUR are opt-in capability members ---\n  1. dispatch_one.py:465  \"TicketTransport\" has no attribute \"add_comment\"       (in create_one)\n  2. dispatch_one.py:667  \"TicketTransport\" has no attribute \"add_comment\"       (in _update_one_scalar_update)\n  3. dispatch_one.py:752  \"TicketTransport\" has no attribute \"set_relationship\"  (in _update_one_dispatch_links)\n  4. dispatch_apply_phases.py:165 \"TicketTransport\" has no attribute \"add_comment\" (in _update_one_dispatch_comments)\n\nRESOLUTION — all four NARROWED to the capability Protocol that declares the member:\n  1,2,4 -> cast(\"SupportsComments\", client).add_comment(...)\n  3     -> _call_with_retry(cast(\"SupportsLinks\", client).set_relationship, ...)\nNONE resolved by widening to Any. NONE by reverting an import to the rebar_reconciler. path. NONE\nby adding the member to TicketTransport's body (that would oblige every future transport to provide\nan opt-in feature). The narrowed value stays attribute-checked — a typo in the member name is still\nan [attr-defined] error — so this is a narrowing, not an escape. The cast target is a STRING forward\nreference, so the SupportsComments/SupportsLinks import stays under TYPE_CHECKING and nothing changes\nat runtime. The rationale is written once, next to the four members, in _backend.py.\n\nAN INDEPENDENT FINDING, worth carrying: NONE of the four sites has a runtime capability guard. The\ncore calls add_comment / set_relationship unconditionally, unlike fetcher.py:553/:590 which do gate\nget_comment_map / get_issuelinks_map behind hasattr. It is safe today only because the differ that\npopulates the mutation is itself capability-gated upstream — an invariant nothing local asserts.\nmypy is the only one of the three checks that can see these sites at all: the AST audit cannot,\nbecause hasattr(TicketTransport, 'add_comment') is True via the metaclass. First concrete\nconfirmation that mypy is strictly STRONGER than the audit here, as the plan predicted.\n\n--- CLASS B: 16 x [assignment] 'Incompatible default for parameter (default has type None)' ---\napplier.py:385; apply_inbound.py:65,133,173,282,309,335,352; apply_outbound.py:91,124,163,183,206;\nbinding_store.py:557; outbound_comments.py:167; typed_dispatch.py:100.\nRESOLUTION: annotated 'TicketTransport | None' — these parameters genuinely default to None and\nevery one of the bodies already guards 'if client is None'. This produced ZERO follow-on errors,\nwhich is itself the evidence that the Optional is honest rather than papered over: had any body\ndereferenced client unconditionally, mypy would have reported it.\n\nAFTER RESOLUTION: mypy src/rebar is clean (413 files). No new type: ignore was added anywhere.\n\n--- SCALE ACTUALLY ANNOTATED ---\n38 port parameters across 14 core modules: 29 were BARE, 9 were annotated 'Any'. (The ticket's\n15/10 sizing counted a narrower slice.) One deliberate Any REMAINS and is pinned by an assertion:\n_backend.assert_transport_conforms(transport: Any) — it is the runtime guard itself, whose job is to\ninspect an object that may NOT conform; annotating it as the port would assert the very property it\nexists to check."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785701888978907001,"uuid":"c27d38fa-5b97-4d55-91fa-980722dca874"}